  <h2 id="the-four-calorie-paradox">The 4-Calorie Paradox: Laboratory Bomb Calorimetry vs. Human Metabolism</h2>
  
  <p>Look at any nutrition facts label in the grocery store, and the standard arithmetic is identical: every single gram of protein yields <strong>4 calories</strong>, and every single gram of carbohydrate yields <strong>4 calories</strong>. This metric stems from the 19th-century Atwater energy system, developed by chemist Wilbur Olin Atwater by burning dried food samples inside a pressurized bomb calorimeter.</p>

  <p>In a stainless-steel combustion chamber, protein and carbohydrates produce an identical thermal explosion of heat energy. However, <em>the human digestive tract is not an industrial furnace</em>. When you chew, swallow, and assimilate nutrients, your body undergoes complex, multi-step enzymatic cascades that consume substantial metabolic energy.</p>

  <p>The gross energy written on the package is not the net metabolizable energy your body actually keeps. Once you factor in the metabolic cost of digestion, cellular assimilation, and hepatic processing, the biological reality looks completely different:</p>

  <div class="not-prose my-8 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="card p-6 border-l-4 border-brand bg-surface">
      <span class="text-xs uppercase font-bold tracking-wider text-brand">Macronutrient: Protein</span>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-1">Net Energy: ~2.8 to 3.2 kcal/g</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-body mt-2">Between <strong>20% and 30%</strong> of the caloric value of protein is immediately dissipated as thermal heat during digestion, peptide cleavage, and urea synthesis. Eating 100 kcal of protein leaves your body with only 70 to 80 usable calories.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card p-6 border-l-4 border-amber-500 bg-surface">
      <span class="text-xs uppercase font-bold tracking-wider text-amber-600">Macronutrient: Carbohydrates</span>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mt-1">Net Energy: ~3.6 to 3.8 kcal/g</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-body mt-2">Only <strong>5% to 10%</strong> of carbohydrate calories are consumed during digestion and glycogen storage. Eating 100 kcal of digestible carbs delivers 90 to 95 usable calories directly into circulation as blood glucose.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

