  <h2 id="energy-delivery-and-athletic-performance">Energy Delivery: Why You Still Need Carbohydrates</h2>

  <p>Given protein's superiority for metabolic burn and appetite suppression, you might wonder: <em>Why not replace all carbohydrates with protein?</em></p>

  <p>While low-carb and ketogenic diets are effective for certain populations, completely eliminating carbohydrates handicaps exercise intensity, muscular recovery, and athletic endurance. Contrary to persistent diet culture myths, <a href="/guides/do-carbs-make-you-fat/" class="text-brand underline font-medium">carbs do not inherently make you fat</a>—they are your neuromuscular system's ultimate high-octane fuel.</p>

  <h3>1. Muscle Glycogen: The Fuel for Anaerobic Power</h3>
  <p>Intense weightlifting, sprinting, and high-intensity interval training (HIIT) rely almost exclusively on the <strong>anaerobic glycolytic energy system</strong>. When you contract a muscle against heavy resistance (above 70% of your 1-rep maximum), your cells cannot oxidize fatty acids or amino acids fast enough to resynthesize Adenosine Triphosphate (ATP). They demand stored intracellular <strong>muscle glycogen</strong>.</p>
  <p>If your glycogen stores are chronically depleted due to severe carbohydrate restriction, your power output, barbell velocity, and total repetitions drop precipitously. Over time, diminished training volume blunts mechanical tension—the number one driver of muscle hypertrophy.</p>

  <h3>2. The "Protein-Sparing" Effect of Carbohydrates</h3>
  <p>When you consume adequate carbohydrates, your body uses circulating glucose and stored glycogen for daily physical energy demands. This frees up dietary amino acids to perform their true, irreplaceable job: structural tissue repair, immune function, and <a href="/guides/body-recomposition-guide/" class="text-brand underline font-medium">muscle protein synthesis (MPS)</a>.</p>
  <p>In the absence of sufficient dietary carbohydrates or fats, your liver is forced to convert valuable amino acids into glucose via <strong>gluconeogenesis</strong>. Burning expensive, muscle-building protein as an energy source is an inefficient metabolic compromise.</p>

  <h2 id="fat-loss-vs-muscle-building">Protein vs. Carbs: Which Wins for Your Specific Goal?</h2>

